0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.0005325074321482893,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":11.7,"_gate_share_shrink":0.21947552497520517,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":0.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.5313099841433221,"_gp_sbc_dilution":false,"_score_sbc_dilution":0.0,"_gate_insider_own":0.00791,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":5.3,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"C4 Therapeutics, Inc., a clinical-stage biopharmaceutical company, develops novel therapeutic candidates to degrade disease-causing proteins. Its lead product candidate is Cemsidomide, an orally bioavailable monodac targeting IKZF1 and IKZF3, or IKZF1/3 to address a need across multiple lines of therapy in multiple myeloma, which has competed its phase 1. The company is also developing oncology product candidate, CFT8919, an orally bioavailable, allosteric, mutant-selective bidac degrader of epidermal growth factor receptor, with an L858R mutation in non-small cell lung cancer; and new degraders focused on inflammation, neuroinflammation, and neurodegeneration. It has strategic collaborations F. Hoffmann-La Roche Ltd and Hoffmann-La Roche Inc., Betta Pharmaceuticals Co. Ltd., and Merck KGAA. Additionally, it has a collaboration agreement with Roche Holding AG for the development of Degrader-Antibody Conjugates for cancer treatment. The company was incorporated in 2015 and is headquartered in Watertown, Massachusetts.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Andrew J. Hirsch M.B.A. | CEO, President & Director | Age 54 | Compensation: $1.4M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 104 people. Revenue per employee of $346K ranks in the below the sector median among Healthcare peers. Stock-based compensation of $184K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 4\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["C4 Therapeutics, Inc. is a small-cap healthcare business with a $362M market cap on $36M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +5% over the past three years, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","The company is burning through $99M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","Shareholder yield is only 0.1%, a thin return of capital, and stock-based comp at 53% of revenue is silent dilution an owner has to account for, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (-1.0) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (35.7% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Operating margins 320% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Patent cliffs, drug-pricing reform, and reimbursement pressure \u2014 a single regulatory change can reset the pricing power that justifies the multiple.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Clinical trial concentration risk \u2014 binary FDA outcomes on a lead asset can wipe out billions in market cap in a single session.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"GLP-1 disruption across sub-sectors \u2014 weight-loss drugs are reshaping demand for orthopaedics, cardiovascular devices, and sleep-apnea treatments in ways not yet priced in.","sev":"amber",